<PubmedArticleSet><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">28877469</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2018</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>14</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2023</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>13</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2211-1247</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>20</Volume><Issue>10</Issue><PubDate><Year>2017</Year><Month>Sep</Month><Day>05</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Cell reports</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Cell Rep</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Autophagy-Independent Lysosomal Targeting Regulated by ULK1/2-FIP200 and ATG9.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>2341</StartPage><EndPage>2356</EndPage><MedlinePgn>2341-2356</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.celrep.2017.08.034</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2211-1247(17)31141-5</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Iron is vital for many homeostatic processes, and its liberation from ferritin nanocages occurs in the lysosome. Studies indicate that ferritin and its binding partner nuclear receptor coactivator-4 (NCOA4) are targeted to lysosomes by a form of selective autophagy. By using genome-scale functional screening, we identify an alternative lysosomal transport pathway for ferritin that requires FIP200, ATG9A, VPS34, and TAX1BP1 but lacks involvement of the ATG8 lipidation machinery that constitutes classical macroautophagy. TAX1BP1 binds directly to NCOA4 and is required for lysosomal trafficking of ferritin under basal and iron-depleted conditions. Under basal conditions ULK1/2-FIP200 controls ferritin turnover, but its deletion leads to TAX1BP1-dependent activation of TBK1 that regulates redistribution of ATG9A to the Golgi enabling continued trafficking of ferritin. Cells expressing an amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)-associated TBK1 allele are incapable of degrading ferritin suggesting a molecular mechanism that explains the presence of iron deposits in patient brain biopsies.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#169; 2017 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Goodwin</LastName><ForeName>Jonathan M</ForeName><Initials>JM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Novartis Institutes for Biomedical Research, 181 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dowdle</LastName><ForeName>William E</ForeName><Initials>WE</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Novartis Institutes for Biomedical Research, 181 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>DeJesus</LastName><ForeName>Rowena</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Novartis Institutes for Biomedical Research, 181 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Zuncai</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Novartis Institutes for Biomedical Research, 181 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bergman</LastName><ForeName>Philip</ForeName><Initials>P</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Novartis Institutes for Biomedical Research, 181 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kobylarz</LastName><ForeName>Marek</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Novartis Institutes for Biomedical Research, 181 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lindeman</LastName><ForeName>Alicia</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Novartis 